  "query_preview": "..A very moist atmosphere for mid-September is in \nplace awaiting the dynamics from a small upper low that was \nsouth of Hermosillo, MX this morning. This feature will swing \nnorth across the area today bringing moderate to heavy rainfall \nto portions of southeast Arizona and the increased chances for \nflash flooding due to moist antecedent conditions. A Flash Flood\nWatch is in effect until 11 pm tonight.\n\nSatellite imagery showed southerly flow across the area which \ncontinues to slowly increase PWAT values into the 1.3\" to 1.8\"\nrange which is 175%-200% of normal. Radar showing initial area\nof showers/storms moving into southern Cochise county. Expect \nareal coverage of showers/storms to slowly increase over the\nnext 2-5 hours before more rapid development occurs later this \nmorning as the upper low edges closer to the Intl border. Hourly\nrainfall rates of 1\"-2.5\" with some of the heavy showers with \nsome storms over 3\". The Weather Prediction Center has a \nModerate risk for excessive rainfall for portions of Pima, \nPinal, and Graham counties today.\n\nThursday: Precipitable water remains elevated for this time of \nyear with a slight risk for excessive rainfall continuing east \nand south of Tucson. Showers and storms that develop will still \nhave the potential for locally heavy rainfall, but without the \ndisturbance nearby there will be a lack of forcing to help aid \nin higher rainfall rates like Wednesday.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: Drier air finally begins to filter into\nsoutheast Arizona as flow aloft shifts more \nwesterly/southwesterly. This could help limit thunderstorm \nactivity somewhat and keep activity mainly east and south of \nTucson. High temperatures return to at or slightly above normal.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Wed Sep 23 to Tue Sep 29)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 39-42% chance\nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal, 39-42% chance",
